				<td width="100%"><a href="viewtopic.php?p=16086&amp;sid=24390e5f6214a7518587f3739f2b316a#16086"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/icon_minipost.gif" width="12" height="9" alt="Post" title="Post" border="0" /></a><span class="postdetails">Posted: Tue Jan 14, 2003 4:04 pm<span class="gen">&nbsp;</span>&nbsp; &nbsp;Post subject: </span></td>
				<td valign="top" align="right" nowrap="nowrap"><a href="posting.php?mode=quote&amp;p=16086&amp;sid=24390e5f6214a7518587f3739f2b316a"><img src="templates/Chronicles/images/lang_english/icon_quote.gif" alt="Reply with quote" title="Reply with quote" border="0" /></a>   </td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><hr /></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">I’m interested in working on the Prayer Shawl Quest. I’ve just recently gotten my skills up to the point that it’s feasible to start.  I also want to do the Cabbage Quest but it’ll be a while before I can use the item.  <img src="images/smiles/icon_smile.gif" alt="icon_smile.gif" border="0" />
<br />

<br />
Here is a skill breakdown of the shawls:
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold"> Shawl 3</span>
<br />
Baking: 118-127 
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold"> Shawl 4</span>
<br />
Pottery: 122-123 
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold"> Shawl 5</span>
<br />
Tailoring: 115 
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold"> Shawl 6</span>
<br />
Tailoring: 204-222 
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold"> Shawl 7</span>
<br />
Brewing: 162 
<br />
Fletching: 168 
<br />
Jewelcraft: 162 
<br />
Pottery: 162 
<br />
Tailoring: 162 
<br />
<span style="font-weight: bold"> Shawl 8</span>
<br />
Brewing: 158 (optional) 
<br />
Jewelcraft: 182 
<br />
Smithing: 187 
<br />
Tailoring: 176
<br />

<br />
A complete walkthrough of the Prayer Shawl Quest can be found at EQ Traders: <a href="http://www.eqtraders.com/quests/intro.htm" target="_blank">http://www.eqtraders.com/quests/intro.htm</a>
<br />

<br />
And a walkthrough for the Cabbage Quest can be found at: <a href="http://www.eqtraders.com/quests/potc_intro.htm" target="_blank">http://www.eqtraders.com/quests/potc_intro.htm</a>
<br />

<br />
It does not make sense that this quest would become NON-MQ since it DOES require grandmastery in multiple tradeskills. The intent of this quest was to have something that guilds or other player groups could do together; was actually REQUIRED to do so. It’s the same thought behind the Beastlord Weapons Quests. Unfortunately the community has not supported this idea very well. Regardless, Brain (don’t remember the last name; one of the main guys on the tradeskill dev. team)  still wants to push to make player cooperate with each other more and sees team tradeskill items as one of the ways to do it.  
<br />

<br />
As a side note to answer a question; “Can’t you only GM 1 tradeskill?”  Yes and no. Yes before you can spend AA points to “uncap” another tradeskill. You pay for each “uncap” separately. Ultimately; you can GM all of them. There are currently only 4 people thought all the servers who have done this (and announced it).
<br />

<br />
Arialana; Potter is the MOST expensive tradeskill to GM. I know it’s kinda shocking to hear that considering what you pay to get to 200 in either jewelcraft or smithing but the cost to go between 220 and 250 is like 85K. After you hit 199 and trivial Casserole dishes each combine after that is a pain and gets very expensive very fast. The general consensus at this time is that baking or brewing are the easiest to GM (and the most profitable at the moment).
<br />

<br />
Oh, and I'm up for the Easter Wastes excursion.    <img src="images/smiles/icon_biggrin.gif" alt="icon_biggrin.gif" border="0" /><br />_________________<br />Morani 
<br />
Wanderer of Tunare, 
<br />
Protector of The Mother's children.</span><span class="gentblsmall"></span></td>
